# Line endings are part of the evaluated input, not a local preference.
#
# scripts/fetch_corpus.py records sha256 of the corpus bytes, and the frozen
# manifest (interface I1) therefore commits one specific byte sequence. On
# Windows the default core.autocrlf=true checkout rewrites those files to CRLF,
# so the same commit passes the hash test on Linux and fails on Windows, and any
# provenance hash computed from a working copy differs per platform. Pinning LF
# keeps data/, the QA csv files and the results snapshots byte-identical
# everywhere, which is what a frozen corpus has to mean.
#
# The QA csv files need it for a second reason: a gold quote may span two source
# lines inside one quoted field, and metrics.load_qa opens the file with
# newline="", so the field keeps whatever line ending the checkout produced while
# the corpus is always read as LF. A CRLF working copy therefore fails verbatim
# matching for those quotes on one platform only - see
# tests/test_eval_metrics.py::test_committed_qa_files_stay_lf_so_multi_line_gold_survives_checkout.
#
# Everything tracked here is plain text; nothing needs a binary rule yet. If a
# model, image or database file is ever committed, mark it `binary` so this rule
# cannot touch its bytes.
* text=auto eol=lf
